    description: "## Origin\n\nCross-language framework-dispatch linker survey 2026-05-07, conducted as a structural-fix scope-expansion question following WI-halat (PR #3539). Surfaced as a \"different-shape gap\" — same family of one-hop traversal limitation that WI-halat identified in framework-dispatch linkers, but in a different consumer (`type_hierarchy.py`) emitting a different edge type.\n\n## Symptom\n\n`packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/type_hierarchy.py` builds a `parent_to_children` map that is one-hop-only, then emits `dispatches_to` edges from each parent's method to its **direct** child overrides. When `Grandparent.foo` is overridden only in `Grandchild` (Parent doesn't override), the `Grandparent.foo → Grandchild.foo` edge is never emitted because Grandchild isn't in `parent_to_children[Grandparent]`.\n\n## Distinction from the framework-dispatch traversal items\n\nThe framework-dispatch items (WI-halat + the broader four-linker item + the grpc.py Go item) all consume `meta.base_classes` for short-name match against framework bases. This item is structurally different:\n\n1. The bug is in **edge emission**, not match traversal: type_hierarchy.py constructs the parent→children map at one hop and never closes it transitively.\n2. The fix shape is different: rather than calling the existing `_transitive_bases` helper at a match site, this requires making `parent_to_children` traversal transitive when iterating override candidates (or pre-closing the map at construction time).\n3. The real-world incidence is likely lower than framework-dispatch transitive miss — skip-level overrides (where an intermediate doesn't override but a leaf does) are a minority pattern compared to typical method-override cascades.\n\n## Fix scope\n\nTwo design options:\n\n(a) **Recursive traversal at iteration time**: when iterating override candidates for `Parent.method`, recurse through `parent_to_children` to collect all transitive descendants, then check each for a matching override.\n\n(b) **Eager transitive closure at construction time**: build a `parent_to_descendants` map that closes `parent_to_children` transitively (BFS with cycle guard, similar to `_transitive_bases`), then iterate descendants in a single flat loop at edge-emission time.\n\nOption (b) likely cleaner — single closure pass, then the edge-emission loop stays simple. Option (a) is locally less invasive. Decide at PR time.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Property test: a three-level chain (`A.foo` defined; `B(A)` does NOT override; `C(B)` overrides) emits `A.foo → C.foo`.\n- Diamond-inheritance cycle is guarded.\n- Existing direct-override tests still pass.\n- 100% coverage on the changed source.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- WI-halat (done, P2): the original Python-only fix introducing `_transitive_bases.py`. Helper-API patterns are reusable but the call site here is structurally distinct.\n- The broader four-linker item: addresses the framework-dispatch within-language gap; this item is the orthogonal type_hierarchy gap.\n- INV-vusog: about *emitting* one-hop extends/implements edges; type_hierarchy consumes those edges. Not about transitive consumer-side walk.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Framework-dispatch linker base-class traversal fixes — separate items (the broader four-linker item + the grpc.py Go item).\n- Option (b) analyzer-side transitive closure of `meta.base_classes` — different fix shape; deferred."  # ef3b
    fields: {}  # ef3b
